s the spectrum, including Republican-affiliated firms, show Kreiss-Tomkins ahead, and he holds a roughly two-and-a-half-to-one fundraising edge over Taylor. Markets price a Democratic win near 85 percent and most raters call the race Lean D, though the state's Republican lean and a divided GOP field keep uncertainty wide.", "watch": "If Republicans consolidate behind a single candidate, whether through Trump's intervention or ranked-choice dynamics, the race would tighten considerably.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike Dunleavy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alaska's governorship is open for the first time in a decade, with Republican Mike Dunleavy term-limited. Democrat Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins faces Republican Treg Taylor, with two other Republicans, Bernadette Wilson and Dave Bronson, still on the ballot. Polls across the spectrum, including surveys from Republican-affiliated firms, show Kreiss-Tomkins leading, and he has out-raised Taylor roughly two-and-a-half to one. Most nonpartisan handicappers rate the race Lean D, though two call it a tossup.\n\nThe main uncertainty is the fragmented Republican field. Trump has publicly called on Taylor to exit the race, and Taylor has refused, raising the prospect of a divided right-of-center vote under Alaska's ranked-choice system. Prediction markets price a Democratic win at 85 to 88 percent, more bullish than the polling alone. A late consolidation behind a single Republican would be the clearest threat to the Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6825, "margin": 2.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7335, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7395, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7331, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7068, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7071, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7313, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7365, "margin": 3.5}, {"date"